    {
        "slug": "power-weeder-vs-power-tiller",
        "title": "Power Weeder vs Power Tiller: Which One Do You Actually Need?",
        "excerpt": "A technical and economic comparison between compact power weeders (5.5\u201310 HP) and heavy walking power tillers (12\u201315 HP) for Indian dealers, FPOs, and commercial growers.",
        "cover_image": "/assets/blog/field-tractor.jpg",
        "tags": ["Power Weeder", "Power Tiller", "Machinery Comparison", "Farm Equipment", "B2B Procurement", "Farm Mechanization"],
        "content": "Farmers and dealers often confuse power weeders with power tillers. Making the wrong procurement choice can result in a machine that is either too heavy for inter-cultivation or too light for primary tillage.\n\n## What is a Power Tiller?\nPower tillers are heavy, two-wheeled walking tractors (typically 12 HP to 15 HP, weighing 150 kg+).\n* **Primary Use:** Primary deep tillage, pulling heavy trailers (1-ton capacity), and massive wetland puddling.\n* **Drawback:** They are too wide and heavy to enter crop rows for inter-cultivation once the crop is growing.\n\n## What is a Power Weeder?\nPower weeders are compact, specialized inter-cultivators (5.5 HP to 10 HP, weighing 65 kg to 135 kg).\n* **Primary Use:** Entering established crop rows (2 ft to 5 ft spacing) to uproot weeds, aerate soil, and earth-up roots.\n* **Advantage:** Highly maneuverable. An operator can work for hours without severe fatigue.\n\n![Weeder vs Tiller](https://cdn.farmingtools.in/weeder-vs-tiller.jpg)\n\n## The Verdict for Dealers & FPOs\nIf your farmers need to replace bullocks for primary plowing and transport, sell them a Power Tiller. If they suffer from labor shortages for *weeding and earthing-up standing crops* like cotton, sugarcane, and vegetables, the **Power Weeder** is the only viable solution.\n",
    },
